WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.602
-Our next guest is
a Grammy Award-winning

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:03.870
singer, songwriter,
and producer

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.005
who is currently nominated
for six Grammy Awards,

00:00:06.005 --> 00:00:07.507
including Producer of the Year.

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:08.975
His band Bleachers

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:11.277
will release their new
self-titled album on March 8th.

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:13.312
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Jack Antonoff,

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:14.313
everybody.

00:00:14.313 --> 00:00:16.315
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.315 --> 00:00:32.999 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:34.600
Welcome back.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.268
How are you, my friend?
-I'm really good.

00:00:36.268 --> 00:00:37.603
-That's great.
I'm glad to hear it.

00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:39.772
-I'm fine.
-I want to talk to you
about stuff,

00:00:39.772 --> 00:00:41.808
but first I -- and I don't
want to belabor this --

00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:44.143
I want to congratulate you
on a few things.

00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:45.511
You pointing at Fred?
-Yeah.

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:47.347
-Is Fred pointing at you?
I don't want to look.

00:00:47.347 --> 00:00:49.816
[ Laughter ]

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:52.118
I have asked him --
I have asked him to stop.

00:00:52.118 --> 00:00:54.787
Oh, boy. [ Laughs ]

00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:55.955
You got married.
Congratulations.

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.657
-Yeah.
-Very exciting.

00:00:57.657 --> 00:00:59.792
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:59.792 --> 00:01:01.494
August.

00:01:03.295 --> 00:01:06.465
Do you feel like
you had enough...?

00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:09.435
Like, I was hard knowing that
this was gonna pass so quickly.

00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:10.770
Did you focus on the night?
Were you like,

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.739
"I'm gonna remember how great
this is the whole time?"

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:15.241
Were you present?
-I think it's for everyone else.

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.610
-Yeah. Interesting.
-And I think that's great.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.112
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:21.347
-Do you feel like as somebody
who performs for a lot of people

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:22.749
all the time,
you were able to be like,

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:24.250
this is just
a performance for them?

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.451
-That's what
I thought would happen.

00:01:25.451 --> 00:01:27.786
-Yeah.
-And then...I...

00:01:27.786 --> 00:01:29.622
was wrong.

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:31.390
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:34.226
Someone told me --
you won't laugh at this --

00:01:34.226 --> 00:01:37.730
that when you die
and when you get married

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.732
is the only time
everyone you love is in a room.

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:41.734
-Mm. Yeah.
[ Light laughter, murmurs ]

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:44.270
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.939
They're like, "We're all dead!"
[ Laughs ]

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:48.474
And I was like,

00:01:48.474 --> 00:01:50.109
oh, that's just a thing
someone says,

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:51.311
and I perform for a living,

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:52.979
so I don't mind
if the attention is on me.

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:56.616
And then as soon as it started,
my whole body became wet.

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.117
-Yeah.

00:01:58.117 --> 00:02:00.987
[ Laughter ]

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:03.656
-It was really beautiful.
I'm looking for jokes,

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.158
but it was, like,
just really beautiful.

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.726
-I'm really glad.
It should be beautiful.

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:09.362
That is a perfect way
for it to be.

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:10.530
-My biggest takeaway is

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.032
this crazy emotional twist
when you....

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:15.234
We all have this, like, armor
that we wear, like,

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:17.837
"Oh, I'm bad at relationships,"
or I'm this or I'm that.

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:20.106
And then if you come
to a point in your life

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:22.608
where that just wasn't true...
-Yeah.

00:02:22.608 --> 00:02:25.177
-...you have to do
so much unpacking of that.

00:02:25.177 --> 00:02:26.345
-Yeah.
-That's a funny thing.

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:27.614
You know?
-Absolutely.

00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:29.182
Where you're like,
"Oh, so it wasn't that.

00:02:29.182 --> 00:02:30.950
It was a different thing."
-Yeah, It was just...

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.052
Yeah, I was just --
not time or whatever.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.887
I don't know,
that's been the big thing

00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:36.655
I'm focused on is sort of like,
oh, for all those years

00:02:36.655 --> 00:02:37.890
I had all this mythology

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.525
and I just, you know,
it hadn't -- it just...

00:02:39.525 --> 00:02:41.059
-Just hadn't met the right
person, that's all.

00:02:41.059 --> 00:02:42.561
-You have a nice wife.

00:02:42.561 --> 00:02:43.896
-I do.
-Yeah.

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:44.897
[ Laughter ]

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:46.165
Okay, I'll say this.

00:02:46.165 --> 00:02:48.200
-You're the first person
who's had this much trouble

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.236
with Fred
being in their eyeline.

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:52.238
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:54.073
-10 seconds to explain it?
-Yeah.

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:57.410
-Me, Fred, Andy Samberg,
Rick Moranis --

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:59.077
we're just a kind of person.

00:02:59.077 --> 00:03:00.580
-Yeah.
-And...

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.615
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:05.150
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.320
-It's true.
-Yeah.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.755
-So this must be great.

00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:10.255
So, when you're in
the same room with him,

00:03:10.255 --> 00:03:12.091
you do feel a need to be like,
"I'm here. You here?"

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:13.459
-I don't feel it,
but, like, other people...

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:14.893
Like, people on the street
will be like,

00:03:14.893 --> 00:03:17.764
"You look like Fred Armisen."
And, yeah.

00:03:17.764 --> 00:03:19.098
Or, like, "You" --

00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:21.601
In a nice way.
It's great.

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:22.935
-The way...

00:03:22.935 --> 00:03:26.505
The way you were making them
say it didn't sound nice.

00:03:26.505 --> 00:03:28.173
I think, like,
on an aura level,

00:03:28.173 --> 00:03:30.776
you also have a similarity,
in a very positive way.

00:03:30.776 --> 00:03:33.780
I like both of your vibes a lot.
Yeah.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.982
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.786
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:03:40.786 --> 00:03:43.956
You know what would be a really
a really funny thing

00:03:43.956 --> 00:03:46.491
to do to you is, like,
when we put this on TV tonight,

00:03:46.491 --> 00:03:48.661
edit in a empty drum kit.

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:50.163
-That would be funny. Yeah.

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:52.031
-So this whole time, like,
Fred isn't here

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:54.801
and it's like we're all,
like, humoring you.

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:59.605
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.605 --> 00:04:01.774
I also, just real quick...

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:03.142
-Sorry. It's about you.
I'm sorry.

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:04.644
-No, no, it's not about...
It's about you.

00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:06.479
I feel like we've made this
about Fred, really,

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:08.214
if we're being honest.
-Yeah.

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:09.549
-But he, you know...
-Yeah.

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:10.783
-He made that bed.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:12.952
-He did.
-Sitting up there.

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:14.320
-Sitting up there
on his high horse.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.455
-Yeah.
Bang, bang, splash, splash.

00:04:16.455 --> 00:04:18.457
-Look at me, look at me.

00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:19.825
Six Grammy nominations.

00:04:19.825 --> 00:04:21.294
Congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:23.128
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:23.128 --> 00:04:25.298
Lana Del Rey album,
Taylor Swift album.

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:28.000
That's some very good work.
Congratulations very much.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.171
-I'm really proud of those two
because I think in my industry,

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:34.373
like, there's this, like,
endless, like, rah-rah,

00:04:34.373 --> 00:04:38.043
pivot this way and that way,
and I've had a few...

00:04:38.043 --> 00:04:39.579
Really, Lana and Taylor,

00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:41.747
we've just worked
for a long period of time

00:04:41.747 --> 00:04:44.183
and, like, found this space
where it's like...

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:46.419
gets like loonier and loonier

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:48.921
and more exciting
and it just makes me feel...

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.689
And it's the same thing
with my band.

00:04:50.689 --> 00:04:54.861
The record we just made, it even
ties into the marriage, too.

00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:57.596
Like, I think a big theme
is, like, investing

00:04:57.596 --> 00:05:00.466
in long relationships.
It makes me feel, like, sweet.

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:02.501
-And doesn't it also mean
that -- because, again,

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:05.571
there is, I think, a -- music
business has a reputation

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:07.573
as everything has to be
fraught and combative.

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:08.841
Do you feel like
you found yourself

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:10.276
in a place when you start
an album with somebody,

00:05:10.276 --> 00:05:11.710
have a long relationship with
where you're like,

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:13.245
this just can be fun?

00:05:13.245 --> 00:05:16.082
-Well, you know, making albums
is hard enough on the mind

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:21.053
and body that it seems like
an embarrassment to introduce,

00:05:21.053 --> 00:05:23.388
like, dissonance
in the actual process.

00:05:23.388 --> 00:05:24.924
-Yeah.
-It's almost like,

00:05:24.924 --> 00:05:26.425
you know, we're fighting

00:05:26.425 --> 00:05:28.427
something so much bigger,
so it seems so crazy.

00:05:28.427 --> 00:05:31.497
It's like we're in a storm to,
like, figure out these things.

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:33.299
So it's so weird
that you would ever be like,

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:35.167
you know,
pick on the person next to you.

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:39.839
I always feel like that's
perpetuated by people who...

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:41.340
[ Sighs ]

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.941
You know, when I was younger
and I always wanted to do music,

00:05:42.941 --> 00:05:45.445
people would always be like,
"Uh, well, that's a tough life."

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:48.480
And it's like, well,
it's all a tough life,

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.282
and it's also
a beautiful life.

00:05:50.282 --> 00:05:53.285
And I wonder which
guidance counselors got together

00:05:53.285 --> 00:05:55.921
and were like, "We're gonna
[bleep] on this job."

00:05:55.921 --> 00:05:57.422
-Yeah.
-You know, 'cause like,

00:05:57.422 --> 00:05:58.957
because it's not
even that weird.

00:05:58.957 --> 00:06:01.327
It's not even like I want
to be, like, flying in space.

00:06:01.327 --> 00:06:03.496
It's like, music is --
there's music everywhere,

00:06:03.496 --> 00:06:04.830
and there's so many jobs
in music,

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:06.198
and there's music here and...

00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:07.566
-Yeah, it's true.

00:06:07.566 --> 00:06:08.935
If somebody walked into
a guidance counselor and said,

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:10.636
"I want to be an astronaut,"
they'd be like, "Great!"

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:11.971
-Exactly! But --
-Even though there's like

00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:12.971
no astronauts.
-There's no jobs.

00:06:12.971 --> 00:06:14.640
If someone wants to, like,

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.008
be an artist or work in music...

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:18.044
Somewhere along the way --
it might be ending,

00:06:18.044 --> 00:06:20.212
but somewhere along the way,
there was this idea of, like,

00:06:20.212 --> 00:06:23.549
"Let's get a plan B."

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:25.718
Or that even if you do it,
your life is horrible.

00:06:25.718 --> 00:06:27.720
Everyone's always like, "I don't
know how you do the traveling."

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.556
And I'm like, "I don't know
how you do the [bleep] sitting."

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:32.558
Yeah, yeah.

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:34.727
-But you don't do that.
Try not to say that.

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:36.595
-I don't. Yeah.
-Yeah, good.

00:06:36.595 --> 00:06:38.263
Leave that to Fred.

00:06:38.263 --> 00:06:40.600
-Yeah.
-That's a classic Fred line.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.268
Last time you were here,
we talked about

00:06:42.268 --> 00:06:44.770
your collaboration
with Bruce Springsteen.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:47.439
And then you did a show
across the street.

00:06:47.439 --> 00:06:48.507
-Yeah.
-Radio City.

00:06:48.507 --> 00:06:49.674
-Why didn't you come?

00:06:49.674 --> 00:06:51.343
-You didn't invite me.

00:06:51.343 --> 00:06:53.078
-Jimmy Fallon came.

00:06:53.078 --> 00:06:55.281
[ Audience ohhs ]

00:06:56.582 --> 00:06:57.916
He just showed up.

00:06:57.916 --> 00:07:00.085
Sorry.
You can cut this part.

00:07:00.085 --> 00:07:03.122
-No, leave it in. Leave it in.
I want them all to know!

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:04.456
[ Laughter ]

00:07:04.456 --> 00:07:05.825
But this is amazing.

00:07:05.825 --> 00:07:08.160
This photo makes me so happy
because I know how much...

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:10.496
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:12.331
...it means to you.

00:07:12.331 --> 00:07:14.200
-The photo is really
speaking a truth

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:18.571
because I'm looking up
and just sort of...

00:07:18.571 --> 00:07:20.506
Yeah, I see myself
being like, like,

00:07:20.506 --> 00:07:22.174
"Thanks for the roadmap"
in the photo.

00:07:22.174 --> 00:07:24.176
-Yeah. No, it is. It is.

00:07:24.176 --> 00:07:25.978
You know, it's nice
because you are --

00:07:25.978 --> 00:07:27.613
you are collaborating
in the photo as well.

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:29.614
Right?
It's not just like a worship.

00:07:29.614 --> 00:07:31.017
You're actually
working together.

00:07:31.017 --> 00:07:32.551
-Yeah.
-But it's like, I don't know,

00:07:32.551 --> 00:07:34.352
it's like you're paying
a nice homage to him there.

00:07:34.352 --> 00:07:36.522
-Well, I mean, he's obviously
one of the great poets

00:07:36.522 --> 00:07:37.723
and musicians of our time,

00:07:37.723 --> 00:07:39.892
but music comes
from a landscape.

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:42.028
I come from a place that --
New Jersey --

00:07:42.028 --> 00:07:44.463
that is very misunderstood,
so you have to...

00:07:44.463 --> 00:07:46.465
[ Laughter, cheers ]

00:07:46.465 --> 00:07:48.467
Literally not a joke.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:48.467 --> 00:07:50.136
-I guess the name
is a punch line.

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:55.140
But, you know, it's you --
you have to -- you need people.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.009
And for me it was Springsteen
and the E Street Band,

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.911
it was Southside Johnny,
it was Saves the Day,

00:07:58.911 --> 00:08:03.749
it was Lifetime,
it was, you know, Lauryn Hill.

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:05.317
There's all these things
that came out of New Jersey

00:08:05.317 --> 00:08:07.052
that said, okay,
like, this place

00:08:07.052 --> 00:08:11.624
that is so misunderstood
has a voice that...

00:08:11.624 --> 00:08:14.526
And specifically with Bruce
in that writing, the landscape,

00:08:14.526 --> 00:08:16.028
it's like, yeah,
it's what we do there.

00:08:16.028 --> 00:08:17.696
We drive on highways and think.

00:08:17.696 --> 00:08:20.533
And it gives you a lot of
courage to write and not,

00:08:20.533 --> 00:08:24.369
um, just drive into the city
and pretend you're from there.

00:08:24.369 --> 00:08:26.872
-That's fantastic.
It's a fantastic story you tell.

00:08:26.872 --> 00:08:29.374
And I'm really happy for all
your great producing work.

00:08:29.374 --> 00:08:31.076
It's really exciting
that Bleachers

00:08:31.076 --> 00:08:32.411
has a new album,
and it's so exciting

00:08:32.411 --> 00:08:33.746
you're gonna do a song
for us tonight.

00:08:33.746 --> 00:08:35.414
-Yeah, first time
we're ever gonna play it.

00:08:35.414 --> 00:08:36.749
-I'm very excited to hear it.

00:08:36.749 --> 00:08:39.051
You guys, Jack Antonoff.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:39.051 --> 00:08:41.888
We'll be right back with
a performance from Bleachers.

